00:03:05.110 --> 00:03:07.470
So I think most of
you know what Kaggle

00:03:07.470 --> 00:03:09.930
is, based on the show of hands.

00:03:09.930 --> 00:03:12.000
The thing that I
first and foremost

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:15.300
think about when I think
about Kaggle is the community.

00:03:15.300 --> 00:03:17.400
It's really a technical
professional community,

00:03:17.400 --> 00:03:19.025
where people who are
entering the space

00:03:19.025 --> 00:03:22.500
or have been working for a while
can make friends, hang out,

00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:26.660
hone their skills, learn
things, share resources.

00:03:26.660 --> 00:03:28.800
And eventually, we want
to be able to support you

00:03:28.800 --> 00:03:33.320
in doing all of your data
science work on Kaggle.

00:03:33.320 --> 00:03:34.530
What's on Kaggle?

00:03:34.530 --> 00:03:35.810
People, lots of people--

00:03:35.810 --> 00:03:37.370
I know I mention
the community a lot.

00:03:37.370 --> 00:03:39.710
But I think Kagglers
are the best.

00:03:39.710 --> 00:03:42.950
We've got over 2.7 million
users at this point.

00:03:42.950 --> 00:03:45.800
I think we have around 300,000
people who log in every month

00:03:45.800 --> 00:03:48.800
to write kernels, or
download data sets,

00:03:48.800 --> 00:03:50.660
or enter competitions.

00:03:50.660 --> 00:03:53.090
We also have 15,000
public data sets.

00:03:53.090 --> 00:03:56.270
So these are data sets
that we've uploaded,

00:03:56.270 --> 00:03:58.280
or mostly that
users have uploaded

00:03:58.280 --> 00:03:59.280
that they want to share.

00:03:59.280 --> 00:04:02.240
So things they've collected,
research data sets,

00:04:02.240 --> 00:04:05.590
and we'll talk a little bit
about some examples of those.

00:04:05.590 --> 00:04:09.410
And we also have a really large
body of scripts and notebooks.

00:04:09.410 --> 00:04:13.220
So this is code for
doing different tasks.

00:04:13.220 --> 00:04:15.170
And you can, of
course, work privately,

00:04:15.170 --> 00:04:17.130
keep your data private,
keep your code private.

00:04:17.130 --> 00:04:20.300
But people really are very
generous with their work

00:04:20.300 --> 00:04:24.740
and share it and walk
people through how to do

00:04:24.740 --> 00:04:26.600
various machine learning tasks.

00:04:26.600 --> 00:04:30.240
So if you're trying to do
something kind of common,

00:04:30.240 --> 00:04:32.882
let's say sentiment analysis
or object detection,

00:04:32.882 --> 00:04:34.840
probably somebody has
already written a kernel.

00:04:34.840 --> 00:04:36.680
And you can go,
give a little upvote

00:04:36.680 --> 00:04:39.067
to-- you want to make sure
you upvote useful kernels.

00:04:39.067 --> 00:04:39.650
And then fork.

00:04:39.650 --> 00:04:40.980
And you don't have to
start from scratch.

00:04:40.980 --> 00:04:43.400
You can use this pipeline
that somebody else has already

00:04:43.400 --> 00:04:45.860
built. And it's a
huge time saver.

00:04:45.860 --> 00:04:48.440
I am always using other
people's Kaggle kernels

00:04:48.440 --> 00:04:49.700
to get my work started.

00:04:52.270 --> 00:04:56.490
So we're probably best known
as a competitions platform.

00:04:56.490 --> 00:04:58.560
That's sort of how
we got our start.

00:04:58.560 --> 00:05:00.850
And we absolutely still do that.

00:05:00.850 --> 00:05:03.830
But these days, Kaggle is a
lot more than competitions.

00:05:03.830 --> 00:05:05.580
And I want to highlight
some of the things

00:05:05.580 --> 00:05:08.430
that we've done recently,
some of the changes.

00:05:08.430 --> 00:05:12.390
So the Kaggle products that
I personally probably use

00:05:12.390 --> 00:05:14.810
the most is kernels.

00:05:14.810 --> 00:05:15.518
What are kernels?

00:05:15.518 --> 00:05:17.268
I mean, they're, like,
18 different things

00:05:17.268 --> 00:05:18.910
in data science and
computer science.

00:05:18.910 --> 00:05:24.030
So Kaggle kernels are a hosted,
in browser, coding environment.

00:05:24.030 --> 00:05:25.920
So we support Jupyter Notebooks.

00:05:25.920 --> 00:05:27.840
That's by far the most popular.

00:05:27.840 --> 00:05:31.110
We support the Python
3 and R kernels.

00:05:31.110 --> 00:05:33.530
We do not support Python 2.

00:05:33.530 --> 00:05:35.130
And we also have scripts.

00:05:35.130 --> 00:05:36.420
So these are flat files.

00:05:36.420 --> 00:05:39.870
And we support Python
R, and also R Markdown.

00:05:39.870 --> 00:05:43.350
So if you're-- any R
Markdown fans out here?

00:05:43.350 --> 00:05:44.250
Yeah!

00:05:44.250 --> 00:05:46.230
I'm a big fan of Markdown.

00:05:46.230 --> 00:05:48.750
So you can write and
run Markdown on Kaggle,

00:05:48.750 --> 00:05:52.515
and then you have a nice link
that you can share with people.

00:05:52.515 --> 00:05:54.720
Or, I mean, of course,
download, and use blog down,

00:05:54.720 --> 00:05:56.910
or book down, or slide down,
or whatever else you're

00:05:56.910 --> 00:05:59.550
using in the ecosystem.

00:05:59.550 --> 00:06:01.995
And we also have, once you
created your kernels, a really

00:06:01.995 --> 00:06:02.870
nice way to share it.

00:06:02.870 --> 00:06:03.890
You make it public.

00:06:03.890 --> 00:06:05.878
You can post about
it on the forums.

00:06:05.878 --> 00:06:07.670
If you haven't been on
the site in a while,

00:06:07.670 --> 00:06:09.980
we now have a
personalized news feed.

00:06:09.980 --> 00:06:12.633
So we've sort of algorithmically
picked some kernels

00:06:12.633 --> 00:06:14.300
that we think might
be exciting for you,

00:06:14.300 --> 00:06:16.890
or competition announcements
we think you might like.

00:06:16.890 --> 00:06:19.100
So you can always see
what's new and happening

00:06:19.100 --> 00:06:22.020
and people are talking about.

00:06:22.020 --> 00:06:25.400
And there's a progression system
for kernels and data sets,

00:06:25.400 --> 00:06:27.410
as well as for competitions.

00:06:27.410 --> 00:06:30.390
So uploading other people's
data sets and kernels--

00:06:30.390 --> 00:06:34.168
sorry, uploading other people's
forum posts and kernels helps

00:06:34.168 --> 00:06:36.210
them progress in the
competition system, and say,

00:06:36.210 --> 00:06:37.880
hey, this community
values my work.

00:06:37.880 --> 00:06:41.450
That's just a nice
thing to learn about,

00:06:41.450 --> 00:06:44.990
that others value your work
or to let people know that you

00:06:44.990 --> 00:06:47.590
appreciate what they do.

00:06:47.590 --> 00:06:50.520
So here is an example
kernel that is a little bit

00:06:50.520 --> 00:06:52.960
small that I wrote.

00:06:52.960 --> 00:06:54.240
And this is an R kernel.

00:06:54.240 --> 00:06:56.280
I like R. I also use Python.

00:06:56.280 --> 00:07:00.150
And this shows you how
to read in a wave file

00:07:00.150 --> 00:07:02.000
and create a spectogram.

00:07:02.000 --> 00:07:03.690
And if you're not
familiar, spectograms

00:07:03.690 --> 00:07:08.040
are a time frequency, intensity
transformation of audio files.

00:07:08.040 --> 00:07:10.470
It's a way to visualize
sounds, basically.

00:07:10.470 --> 00:07:15.330
And this shows you the sounds
of ground parrots, which

00:07:15.330 --> 00:07:19.080
are an Australian parrot
that lives on the ground.

00:07:19.080 --> 00:07:20.790
And a bioacoustics
researcher just

00:07:20.790 --> 00:07:23.250
uploaded a data set
of calls that they've

00:07:23.250 --> 00:07:24.550
recorded from these parrots.

00:07:24.550 --> 00:07:26.592
Now it's, like, I've got
an acoustics background.

00:07:26.592 --> 00:07:28.240
I think that's kind
of interesting.

00:07:28.240 --> 00:07:30.810
So I've just whipped up
a little visualization

00:07:30.810 --> 00:07:34.000
to show what they looked like.

00:07:34.000 --> 00:07:36.380
And if somebody else
wanted to do spectograms,

00:07:36.380 --> 00:07:39.800
we do have a sound-based
competition going on right now.

00:07:39.800 --> 00:07:43.680
You could copy this
kernel, fork this kernel,

00:07:43.680 --> 00:07:45.710
read it in a different
data set, remove

00:07:45.710 --> 00:07:47.270
the data set that
I'd used and just

00:07:47.270 --> 00:07:49.160
reapply this code
to a new data set.

00:07:52.810 --> 00:07:55.480
So I mentioned collaborations
and discussions.

00:07:55.480 --> 00:07:58.900
Our forums are super
duper duper active.

00:07:58.900 --> 00:08:01.600
It's a really great place
to ask technical questions

00:08:01.600 --> 00:08:03.608
to a community of
other data scientists.

00:08:03.608 --> 00:08:05.650
And of course, there's
lots of places to do that.

00:08:05.650 --> 00:08:06.608
There's Stack Overflow.

00:08:06.608 --> 00:08:08.200
There's Stack Exchange.

00:08:08.200 --> 00:08:10.630
But particularly around machine
learning, deep learning,

00:08:10.630 --> 00:08:12.713
machine learning engineering,
the Kaggle community

00:08:12.713 --> 00:08:15.020
is really great and supportive.

00:08:15.020 --> 00:08:17.860
And we also have events.

00:08:17.860 --> 00:08:21.820
So some of those are in person,
and some of those are digital.

00:08:21.820 --> 00:08:26.020
So an example of an in-person
event is Kaggle Days.

00:08:26.020 --> 00:08:28.660
This is a picture
of everybody who

00:08:28.660 --> 00:08:31.660
went to Kaggle Days Paris,
which was a little while ago.

00:08:31.660 --> 00:08:33.850
We're currently actually
having Kaggle Days

00:08:33.850 --> 00:08:36.730
right now in conjunction with
Next, which I'm afraid is full.

00:08:36.730 --> 00:08:41.299
So if you didn't sign up ahead
of time, catch us next year.

00:08:41.299 --> 00:08:42.760
And it's been really great.

00:08:42.760 --> 00:08:44.410
We've gotten talks
by grand masters.

00:08:44.410 --> 00:08:45.710
People have run workshops.

00:08:45.710 --> 00:08:47.500
We're doing a little
on site competition

00:08:47.500 --> 00:08:51.550
that people are working on
right now with manufacturing,

00:08:51.550 --> 00:08:54.160
prediction, defect detection.

00:08:54.160 --> 00:08:55.660
And it's been a
really fantastic way

00:08:55.660 --> 00:08:57.010
to meet people face-to-face.

00:08:57.010 --> 00:08:59.447
Because you spend so much time
chatting with them online.

00:08:59.447 --> 00:09:01.780
Or maybe you've just seen
them going past in the forums,

00:09:01.780 --> 00:09:03.155
and you're like,
oh, I don't know

00:09:03.155 --> 00:09:04.510
if I could ever approach them.

00:09:04.510 --> 00:09:06.802
And it's been really great
to meet people face-to-face.

00:09:06.802 --> 00:09:09.700
So if you interested in coming
and hanging out with Kagglers,

00:09:09.700 --> 00:09:12.940
keep an eye out for Kaggle Days.

00:09:12.940 --> 00:09:14.660
And we also have online events.

00:09:14.660 --> 00:09:17.955
And we know that we
have a lot of users

00:09:17.955 --> 00:09:20.080
in different parts of the
world for whom it may not

00:09:20.080 --> 00:09:24.450
be reasonable to get to,
say, Paris or San Francisco.

00:09:24.450 --> 00:09:26.950
So we really want to make sure
that we're reaching everybody

00:09:26.950 --> 00:09:28.367
and we're really
being accessible.

00:09:28.367 --> 00:09:30.850
And even if you are in the US,
and you have small children

00:09:30.850 --> 00:09:32.433
and can't travel,
we want to make sure

00:09:32.433 --> 00:09:34.240
that you can still
grow professionally

00:09:34.240 --> 00:09:35.592
as a data scientist.

00:09:35.592 --> 00:09:38.050
So one of the things that we're
doing next week, actually--

00:09:38.050 --> 00:09:39.430
you can still sign up for this--

00:09:39.430 --> 00:09:40.720
is CareerCon.

00:09:40.720 --> 00:09:42.910
And this is a
completely digital event

00:09:42.910 --> 00:09:46.060
to help you land your
first data science job.

00:09:46.060 --> 00:09:48.730
So I'm doing an educational
event as part of this

00:09:48.730 --> 00:09:51.280
to help people who've
never made an API make

00:09:51.280 --> 00:09:52.420
an API from scratch.

00:09:52.420 --> 00:09:54.070
We're going to talk
about open API.

00:09:54.070 --> 00:09:55.510
You may know it as Swagger.

00:09:55.510 --> 00:09:56.890
We're going to build
a little [INAUDIBLE]..

00:09:56.890 --> 00:09:57.670
We're going to serve it.

00:09:57.670 --> 00:09:59.330
We're going to go
through the process.

00:09:59.330 --> 00:10:01.120
So you can have
something to show

00:10:01.120 --> 00:10:03.845
on your portfolio or your
resume, saying, hey, sure.

00:10:03.845 --> 00:10:04.720
I'm a data scientist.

00:10:04.720 --> 00:10:06.110
And I'm really good
at machine learning.

00:10:06.110 --> 00:10:07.900
But also I know a
little bit about what

00:10:07.900 --> 00:10:09.070
software engineers do.

00:10:09.070 --> 00:10:11.470
And I can play well with
them and my new teammates.

00:10:11.470 --> 00:10:13.053
And you should hire
me, and I'm great.

00:10:15.960 --> 00:10:20.380
Data-- I was actually originally
hired to be on the data

00:10:20.380 --> 00:10:21.570
sets team.

00:10:21.570 --> 00:10:23.430
And we used to, like
the Kaggle team,

00:10:23.430 --> 00:10:25.800
individually go and find
interesting data sets

00:10:25.800 --> 00:10:28.830
under open licenses and
upload them to Kaggle.

00:10:28.830 --> 00:10:31.710
We don't need to do that anymore
because our community has been

00:10:31.710 --> 00:10:35.310
so, so, so fantastic about
sharing data, research

00:10:35.310 --> 00:10:37.025
data, just people
who have questions

00:10:37.025 --> 00:10:39.358
that they would like to get
in front of machine learning

00:10:39.358 --> 00:10:42.640
researchers that they've
collected the data for.

00:10:42.640 --> 00:10:44.920
And if you have data that
you would like to share,

00:10:44.920 --> 00:10:50.220
I would encourage you to put it
on Kaggle, assuming that we can

00:10:50.220 --> 00:10:51.400
support your specific needs.

00:10:51.400 --> 00:10:53.400
For example, we cannot
support HIPAA compliance.

00:10:53.400 --> 00:10:55.680
Please don't give
us HIPAA data--

00:10:55.680 --> 00:10:57.800
yet.

00:10:57.800 --> 00:10:59.420
So here are some
example data sets

00:10:59.420 --> 00:11:02.610
that the team actually
automatically maintains.

00:11:02.610 --> 00:11:04.310
And these are open
data sets that we

00:11:04.310 --> 00:11:07.820
think might be useful
in your day-to-day work.

00:11:07.820 --> 00:11:11.150
So some things-- there is one
in here that San Francisco

00:11:11.150 --> 00:11:13.350
restaurant scores, like
their health scores.

00:11:13.350 --> 00:11:15.020
So if you're trying to
decide where to go for dinner

00:11:15.020 --> 00:11:16.603
and you want to do
a quick little data

00:11:16.603 --> 00:11:18.680
analysis to make sure
you pick the right place,

00:11:18.680 --> 00:11:22.190
that might be something
you can work on.

00:11:22.190 --> 00:11:24.160
But we've also had some
really fantastic user

00:11:24.160 --> 00:11:25.480
uploaded data sets.

00:11:25.480 --> 00:11:28.450
So you guys might be
familiar with kmnist.

00:11:28.450 --> 00:11:30.650
It was uploaded by a
couple of our users,

00:11:30.650 --> 00:11:35.230
including [INAUDIBLE],, who's our
youngest grandmaster currently.

00:11:35.230 --> 00:11:37.210
[INAUDIBLE] see you.

00:11:37.210 --> 00:11:40.000
And it is a drag
and drop replacement

00:11:40.000 --> 00:11:43.840
for mnist, which is a
digit recognition data set.

00:11:43.840 --> 00:11:46.720
Those of you who are
not familiar with mnist?

00:11:46.720 --> 00:11:47.300
Yes.

00:11:47.300 --> 00:11:47.800
OK.

00:11:47.800 --> 00:11:51.250
So it's a data set for
recognizing handwritten digits

00:11:51.250 --> 00:11:53.480
like one through zero.

00:11:53.480 --> 00:11:57.280
This is the same thing, but
for Japanese classical texts.

00:11:57.280 --> 00:11:59.530
So this team put
together this data set.

00:11:59.530 --> 00:12:00.697
They released it on Kaggle.

00:12:00.697 --> 00:12:02.530
And they actually
presented a paper about it

00:12:02.530 --> 00:12:05.560
at NurIPS, which is
what NIPS is called now.

00:12:05.560 --> 00:12:08.020
I'm not saying putting data
sets on Kaggle will get you

00:12:08.020 --> 00:12:09.127
NurIPS papers.

00:12:09.127 --> 00:12:11.710
But it's a really good place to
find interesting research data

00:12:11.710 --> 00:12:14.127
sets that specifically tailor
towards the machine learning

00:12:14.127 --> 00:12:16.090
community, which is fantastic.

00:12:16.090 --> 00:12:18.430
We also had,
relatively recently,

00:12:18.430 --> 00:12:24.580
some farmer who wanted to
train a drone to spritz

00:12:24.580 --> 00:12:25.510
what's it called--

00:12:25.510 --> 00:12:28.040
not pesticide, but
like for plants.

00:12:28.040 --> 00:12:28.760
Fertilizer?

00:12:28.760 --> 00:12:29.937
No, the other one--

00:12:29.937 --> 00:12:30.770
AUDIENCE: Herbicide?

00:12:30.770 --> 00:12:31.937
RACHAEL TATMAN: --herbicide.

00:12:31.937 --> 00:12:35.708
Thank you, herbicide, a
specific weed that he had.

00:12:35.708 --> 00:12:37.250
So he'd gone to his
field and he took

00:12:37.250 --> 00:12:39.800
a bunch of pictures of the
specific weed he wanted

00:12:39.800 --> 00:12:42.020
to target and uploaded
them so that people

00:12:42.020 --> 00:12:46.560
could try their hand at a really
fun practical application.

00:12:46.560 --> 00:12:48.470
So there's lots to
discover in data sets.

00:12:48.470 --> 00:12:50.053
I love just, like,
going through there

00:12:50.053 --> 00:12:53.200
and seeing what
people have uploaded.

00:12:53.200 --> 00:12:57.350
And finally, Kaggle is a great
place to challenge yourself.

00:12:57.350 --> 00:12:58.820
Of course, we have competitions.

00:12:58.820 --> 00:13:01.860
They are, as you might
imagine, competitive.

00:13:01.860 --> 00:13:03.600
We've got many
going on right now.

00:13:03.600 --> 00:13:05.252
We've got image, using text.

00:13:05.252 --> 00:13:06.960
We've got a sound one
going on right now.

00:13:06.960 --> 00:13:09.440
So whatever it is you're
interested in, as long as it's

00:13:09.440 --> 00:13:11.630
supervised, we'll
probably have something

00:13:11.630 --> 00:13:14.210
that might tickle your fancy.

00:13:14.210 --> 00:13:17.660
We also have a lot of support
for people who are learning,

00:13:17.660 --> 00:13:20.240
who are newer to the data
science space, who maybe just

00:13:20.240 --> 00:13:23.090
aren't compelled to compete or
don't find that particularly

00:13:23.090 --> 00:13:24.350
motivating.

00:13:24.350 --> 00:13:26.720
I personally tend to be more
motivated by finding out

00:13:26.720 --> 00:13:29.160
new things than
doing, like, you know,

00:13:29.160 --> 00:13:31.500
oh, got to get that
last hundredths

00:13:31.500 --> 00:13:33.300
of a digit of accuracy.

00:13:33.300 --> 00:13:37.210
And that's just, you know,
my personal learning style.

00:13:37.210 --> 00:13:39.100
So here is an
example competition.

00:13:39.100 --> 00:13:40.830
I know I talked about
CareerCon a lot,

00:13:40.830 --> 00:13:43.368
but I'm really
excited, you guys.

00:13:43.368 --> 00:13:44.910
Here's a competition
that we're going

00:13:44.910 --> 00:13:49.392
to be doing as part of
CareerCon on robot navigation.

00:13:49.392 --> 00:13:51.600
So if you're interested in
sort of agent-based stuff,

00:13:51.600 --> 00:13:54.960
this might be one for you
to take a little look at.

00:13:54.960 --> 00:14:00.290
And we also have a growing
number of Learn courses.

00:14:00.290 --> 00:14:03.370
And these are not going
to take you completely

00:14:03.370 --> 00:14:05.440
through the math in
a very detailed way.

00:14:05.440 --> 00:14:08.080
Our goal with Learn is to
get you started very quickly

00:14:08.080 --> 00:14:09.560
with practical examples.

00:14:09.560 --> 00:14:11.310
So these are short,
and they're condensed.

00:14:11.310 --> 00:14:12.580
And we want you to be
able to go through them

00:14:12.580 --> 00:14:14.620
in, say, an afternoon
and then be able to apply

00:14:14.620 --> 00:14:15.920
what you've learned.

00:14:15.920 --> 00:14:18.340
So this is one that's
relatively new on machine

00:14:18.340 --> 00:14:22.008
learning explainability that
Dan Becker recently released.

00:14:22.008 --> 00:14:24.050
So if that's something
that you're interested in,

00:14:24.050 --> 00:14:26.217
sit down, spend a few hours,
work through some hands

00:14:26.217 --> 00:14:28.130
on examples, and know more--

00:14:28.130 --> 00:14:28.630
maybe.

00:14:28.630 --> 00:14:29.920
Unless you're already
researching machine

00:14:29.920 --> 00:14:32.410
learning acceptability, in
which case I don't know why

00:14:32.410 --> 00:14:35.480
you'd take a course in it.

00:14:35.480 --> 00:14:35.980
OK.

00:14:35.980 --> 00:14:37.730
So that's what's
on the platform.

00:14:37.730 --> 00:14:41.590
I also want to talk about some
of our Google integrations

00:14:41.590 --> 00:14:43.400
that we've already added.

00:14:43.400 --> 00:14:45.735
And something that I was doing
as I was putting together

00:14:45.735 --> 00:14:47.110
this talk was
really sitting down

00:14:47.110 --> 00:14:49.630
and thinking, how
will these be helpful?

00:14:49.630 --> 00:14:51.790
What specific people
can I see really

00:14:51.790 --> 00:14:53.440
benefiting from
these integrations?

00:14:53.440 --> 00:14:55.607
Because we want to make
sure that we're doing things

00:14:55.607 --> 00:14:58.930
that people can take with
them and enjoy and improve

00:14:58.930 --> 00:15:01.380
their work with.

00:15:01.380 --> 00:15:04.180
So three things that we've
added relatively recently--

00:15:04.180 --> 00:15:07.920
one is an integration
with DataStudio.

00:15:07.920 --> 00:15:11.470
Who has used DataStudio--

00:15:11.470 --> 00:15:13.640
OK, a fair number of people.

00:15:13.640 --> 00:15:17.140
We've also added in
integration with BigQuery.

00:15:17.140 --> 00:15:19.450
And also Google Sheets,
which I'm actually

00:15:19.450 --> 00:15:21.370
very excited about, and
I'll talk about why.

00:15:21.370 --> 00:15:24.790
I know people like to dunk
on spreadsheet software.

00:15:24.790 --> 00:15:26.170
But it's a great tool, you guys.

00:15:26.170 --> 00:15:27.510
Spreadsheets are great.

00:15:27.510 --> 00:15:31.020
All right-- DataStudio--

00:15:31.020 --> 00:15:35.340
so we have a little gif here
that's apparently starting

00:15:35.340 --> 00:15:38.700
in the middle for some reason.

00:15:38.700 --> 00:15:39.360
Interesting.

00:15:39.360 --> 00:15:42.900
So you can click on
any Kaggle data set

00:15:42.900 --> 00:15:46.200
and launch, with just
a couple button clicks

00:15:46.200 --> 00:15:47.770
once you've connected
your DataStudio

00:15:47.770 --> 00:15:52.640
account, a DataStudio
dashboard based on that data.

00:15:52.640 --> 00:15:54.390
If you're not familiar
with it, DataStudio

00:15:54.390 --> 00:15:57.570
is a way to visually
analyze data.

00:15:57.570 --> 00:16:02.190
It's really nice for creating
dashboards, in particular.

00:16:02.190 --> 00:16:05.490
And one use case where I think
this is going to be a huge time

00:16:05.490 --> 00:16:07.680
saver is for people
who are working

00:16:07.680 --> 00:16:09.340
in educational settings.

00:16:09.340 --> 00:16:11.280
So particularly if
you want your students

00:16:11.280 --> 00:16:15.940
to really analyze a specific
data set, just as an example,

00:16:15.940 --> 00:16:18.180
the Arizona Secretary
of State releases

00:16:18.180 --> 00:16:20.760
their data set of
election data as they

00:16:20.760 --> 00:16:22.350
finish cleaning them on Kaggle.

00:16:22.350 --> 00:16:23.970
So if you're a
government professor

00:16:23.970 --> 00:16:26.178
and you want your students
to spend some time looking

00:16:26.178 --> 00:16:28.090
at election data--

00:16:28.090 --> 00:16:30.810
how many of you have done
mapping, or like gif stuff

00:16:30.810 --> 00:16:32.013
in Python or R?

00:16:32.013 --> 00:16:32.743
Yeah.

00:16:32.743 --> 00:16:34.410
Is it, like, fast and
easy and something

00:16:34.410 --> 00:16:36.690
you can do in 10
minutes usually?

00:16:36.690 --> 00:16:37.650
Maybe some of you can.

00:16:37.650 --> 00:16:40.290
But me, I'm always, like, oh,
I've got to pick a projection.

00:16:40.290 --> 00:16:42.000
Oh, no, there's no FIPS code.

00:16:42.000 --> 00:16:44.200
I've got to go get the
FIPs code from somewhere.

00:16:44.200 --> 00:16:45.720
Or oh, I've got a
hook up a mapping

00:16:45.720 --> 00:16:47.550
API because I don't
have, like, OpenStreet

00:16:47.550 --> 00:16:49.260
on whatever I'm doing.

00:16:49.260 --> 00:16:52.170
So I think this could be a
really fast way, particularly

00:16:52.170 --> 00:16:53.040
for mapping.

00:16:53.040 --> 00:16:54.540
Especially if you
want your students

00:16:54.540 --> 00:16:57.710
to be focusing on the
information in the map and not

00:16:57.710 --> 00:17:01.110
the going through
of the mapping.

00:17:01.110 --> 00:17:04.020
Also, I mentioned dashboards.

00:17:04.020 --> 00:17:04.968
I love Jupyter.

00:17:04.968 --> 00:17:06.510
There are some things
that Jupyter is

00:17:06.510 --> 00:17:08.460
really great for dashboarding.

00:17:08.460 --> 00:17:11.640
There are some things where
it's a little bit less good.

00:17:11.640 --> 00:17:13.950
So something that-- actually,
if there's any front end

00:17:13.950 --> 00:17:17.220
people in here who would like to
contribute to Jupyter, there's

00:17:17.220 --> 00:17:19.402
an HTML widgets bug that I
don't think the core team

00:17:19.402 --> 00:17:20.819
is going to get
to because they're

00:17:20.819 --> 00:17:21.990
focusing on Jupyter lab.

00:17:21.990 --> 00:17:23.700
It's been around for a while.

00:17:23.700 --> 00:17:26.730
And every so often, different
plots will render as one pixel

00:17:26.730 --> 00:17:27.390
by one pixel.

00:17:27.390 --> 00:17:29.143
Has anyone run into this?

00:17:29.143 --> 00:17:31.810
Anyway, when you're working with
interactive plotting libraries,

00:17:31.810 --> 00:17:33.060
that can happen.

00:17:33.060 --> 00:17:36.700
And if you run into that, again,
it's just in the Jupyter core

00:17:36.700 --> 00:17:39.310
and there's nothing I personally
can do about it, because I'm

00:17:39.310 --> 00:17:40.990
not a front end person.

00:17:40.990 --> 00:17:43.450
If you are, consider making
open source contributions

00:17:43.450 --> 00:17:45.700
on this particular
bug that bothers me.

00:17:45.700 --> 00:17:49.190
But using Datalab instead is a
nice way to work around that.

00:17:49.190 --> 00:17:52.000
And it's also nice for your
teammates who are maybe not so

00:17:52.000 --> 00:17:53.890
comfortable working with code.

00:17:53.890 --> 00:17:56.170
I know that a lot of us
work in teams with people

00:17:56.170 --> 00:17:58.730
with a variety of
backgrounds and goals.

00:17:58.730 --> 00:18:01.990
So this is a nice way to
reach out and work with them.

00:18:04.550 --> 00:18:07.800
BigQuery-- who uses BigQuery?

00:18:07.800 --> 00:18:08.300
Oh, yeah?

00:18:08.300 --> 00:18:09.467
OK, a fair number of people.

00:18:09.467 --> 00:18:13.650
Who uses any relational
database or SQL, in general--

00:18:13.650 --> 00:18:17.040
yeah, OK, pretty much everybody.

00:18:17.040 --> 00:18:21.740
So right now you can access
public BigQuery data sets

00:18:21.740 --> 00:18:23.840
through Kaggle kernels.

00:18:23.840 --> 00:18:26.185
And we've got some very
small code examples.

00:18:26.185 --> 00:18:28.560
I don't know why I thought
these screens would be bigger.

00:18:28.560 --> 00:18:30.393
I think I thought the
room would be smaller,

00:18:30.393 --> 00:18:32.270
so everyone would be
a little bit closer.

00:18:32.270 --> 00:18:36.950
So you can create a BigQuery
client and run queries--

00:18:36.950 --> 00:18:38.830
wow, I should have
had a second copy--

00:18:38.830 --> 00:18:41.510
and run queries from
directly in Kaggle Notebooks.

00:18:41.510 --> 00:18:44.690
And then take that data and work
with it locally, save it out

00:18:44.690 --> 00:18:46.640
as another data set.

00:18:46.640 --> 00:18:49.520
So if you save any files
from a Jupyter Notebook, when

00:18:49.520 --> 00:18:52.108
you commit that notebook,
it'll run it top to bottom.

00:18:52.108 --> 00:18:53.900
And your files will be
saved in the output,

00:18:53.900 --> 00:18:56.370
and you can create a
new data set from those.

00:18:56.370 --> 00:18:58.850
So if you're running a
BigQuery, or maybe you

00:18:58.850 --> 00:19:00.830
have a teammate who's
not as familiar with SQL,

00:19:00.830 --> 00:19:04.090
but they want to use some
of the BigQuery public data,

00:19:04.090 --> 00:19:05.090
you can run the queries.

00:19:05.090 --> 00:19:06.620
Create a data set from them.

00:19:06.620 --> 00:19:09.720
And then point them to the
data set, which is really nice.

00:19:13.580 --> 00:19:15.320
And if you are
perhaps the person

00:19:15.320 --> 00:19:17.900
who is, like, I don't like SQL,
or maybe it's been a minute

00:19:17.900 --> 00:19:21.207
since you used it, we
also have a SQL course.

00:19:21.207 --> 00:19:22.790
Again, very directed--
we're not going

00:19:22.790 --> 00:19:26.110
to get into, like, the parse
trees of SQL and [INAUDIBLE]..

00:19:26.110 --> 00:19:27.280
We're not doing any of that.

00:19:27.280 --> 00:19:29.660
It's just basics
of query writing

00:19:29.660 --> 00:19:31.913
and specifically, talking
a little bit about BigQuery

00:19:31.913 --> 00:19:33.080
and how to use it on Kaggle.

00:19:33.080 --> 00:19:34.640
So if you're a
little trepidatious

00:19:34.640 --> 00:19:37.685
about getting started, we
can help you out with that

00:19:37.685 --> 00:19:39.150
and walk you through it.

00:19:39.150 --> 00:19:41.930
So just for those of
you who didn't know,

00:19:41.930 --> 00:19:44.240
we have a Kaggle
developer survey

00:19:44.240 --> 00:19:47.090
that we reach out to the machine
learning community in large

00:19:47.090 --> 00:19:49.070
and sort of get
information back.

00:19:49.070 --> 00:19:52.220
And SQL is consistently the
third most popular language

00:19:52.220 --> 00:19:55.153
after Python and R. So if
you are not a SQL user,

00:19:55.153 --> 00:19:57.070
and you're working the
machine learning space,

00:19:57.070 --> 00:19:59.930
it's good to know.

00:19:59.930 --> 00:20:02.510
Finally, Google Sheets--

00:20:02.510 --> 00:20:04.690
I really like
spreadsheet software.

00:20:04.690 --> 00:20:05.940
I think I mentioned it before.

00:20:05.940 --> 00:20:10.668
I think it's a really nice tool
set that makes a lot of things

00:20:10.668 --> 00:20:12.710
very accessible to people,
because you don't have

00:20:12.710 --> 00:20:15.650
the cognitive overhead
of trying to remember

00:20:15.650 --> 00:20:19.020
what data structures look like
because it's there visually.

00:20:19.020 --> 00:20:22.460
So I know people [INAUDIBLE]
on spreadsheet software.

00:20:22.460 --> 00:20:23.840
But I'm a big fan.

00:20:23.840 --> 00:20:30.600
So to open data sets in a
Google Sheet, you can do that--

00:20:30.600 --> 00:20:32.180
I don't know if
you guys can see--

00:20:32.180 --> 00:20:34.940
it's under that little sort
of hamburger, three-dot menu

00:20:34.940 --> 00:20:36.630
in the upper right-hand corner.

00:20:36.630 --> 00:20:39.050
And you can open it
as a Google Sheet.

00:20:39.050 --> 00:20:40.250
It takes just a second.

00:20:40.250 --> 00:20:42.990
And then it'll pop open.

00:20:42.990 --> 00:20:45.060
So two places where
I think that this

00:20:45.060 --> 00:20:48.600
might be a sort of
a surprising use

00:20:48.600 --> 00:20:51.130
is first for project management.

00:20:51.130 --> 00:20:52.080
So if you've got--

00:20:52.080 --> 00:20:55.050
I mentioned that you can create
a data set from the output

00:20:55.050 --> 00:20:56.970
files you generate
in any kernel.

00:20:56.970 --> 00:20:59.478
So let's say you're working
with some competition teammates,

00:20:59.478 --> 00:21:01.770
you're sort of deciding what
your hyperparameter search

00:21:01.770 --> 00:21:02.645
space is going to be.

00:21:02.645 --> 00:21:05.340
You generate a file with
sort of all the parameters.

00:21:05.340 --> 00:21:07.867
You create a data set from
it, launch it as a sheet,

00:21:07.867 --> 00:21:09.450
and then you can use
that as your sort

00:21:09.450 --> 00:21:10.950
of base of operations.

00:21:10.950 --> 00:21:13.320
Color code who's doing what.

00:21:13.320 --> 00:21:14.398
You can ping people.

00:21:14.398 --> 00:21:16.440
So you can be, like, this
action item is for you.

00:21:16.440 --> 00:21:19.720
And they'll get an email,
which is really nice.

00:21:19.720 --> 00:21:21.565
So it can be a nice
project management tool.

00:21:21.565 --> 00:21:23.940
Because I know that that's
something that a lot of people

00:21:23.940 --> 00:21:26.400
struggle with and I see a lot of
discussion about on the forum.

00:21:26.400 --> 00:21:27.690
So if you don't already
have one and you're

00:21:27.690 --> 00:21:29.220
working with other
people, Sheets

00:21:29.220 --> 00:21:31.570
might be a nice integration.

00:21:31.570 --> 00:21:32.613
And a really hacky one--

00:21:32.613 --> 00:21:33.280
I'm sorry, guys.

00:21:33.280 --> 00:21:34.450
This is extremely hacky.

00:21:34.450 --> 00:21:36.530
But I think would be useful.

00:21:36.530 --> 00:21:40.100
So one of the ways that people
do text data augmentations

00:21:40.100 --> 00:21:44.620
who have a small data set, how
can you create new data items

00:21:44.620 --> 00:21:45.340
for images.

00:21:45.340 --> 00:21:47.140
You can flip it and rotate it.

00:21:47.140 --> 00:21:48.820
What does it mean
to flip a sentence?

00:21:48.820 --> 00:21:51.580
It sort of stops
becoming language.

00:21:51.580 --> 00:21:53.260
One way that people
do this is they'll

00:21:53.260 --> 00:21:55.990
translate from English
into an unrelated language,

00:21:55.990 --> 00:21:58.300
like Japanese or Russian,
and then back into English.

00:21:58.300 --> 00:22:00.467
Because you'll get sort of,
like, slightly different

00:22:00.467 --> 00:22:02.985
sentences that are kind of
semantically equivalent.

00:22:02.985 --> 00:22:04.610
And I don't know if
you guys know this,

00:22:04.610 --> 00:22:08.440
but there's actually a Google
translate option in Sheets now.

00:22:08.440 --> 00:22:11.040
So one way to do this
would be to create a data

00:22:11.040 --> 00:22:13.770
set of the text data you
want, open it in Sheets,

00:22:13.770 --> 00:22:15.520
translate it using
Sheets, and then create

00:22:15.520 --> 00:22:17.620
a new data set from your
translated sentences

00:22:17.620 --> 00:22:20.140
for your data augmentation.

00:22:20.140 --> 00:22:23.350
I did warn you it was hacky, but
potentially, very useful if you

00:22:23.350 --> 00:22:27.250
need to do text augmentation.

00:22:27.250 --> 00:22:30.160
So those are some
of the integrations

00:22:30.160 --> 00:22:32.770
that we have worked on so far.

00:22:32.770 --> 00:22:34.070
More to come--

00:22:34.070 --> 00:22:35.932
TM, veil of secrecy.

00:22:35.932 --> 00:22:37.390
But there's lots
of exciting things

00:22:37.390 --> 00:22:39.460
that we're working on
that I think you'll really

00:22:39.460 --> 00:22:41.920
like and find useful.

00:22:41.920 --> 00:22:44.440
And we really want to
make Kaggle a great place

00:22:44.440 --> 00:22:45.680
to do data science.

00:22:45.680 --> 00:22:47.875
So I encourage you,
during the Q&amp;A--

00:22:47.875 --> 00:22:49.000
I haven't checked the Dory.

00:22:49.000 --> 00:22:51.530
Hopefully, everyone's
asking questions.

00:22:51.530 --> 00:22:52.738
So let us know your feedback.

00:22:52.738 --> 00:22:54.530
If there's specific
things you want to see,

00:22:54.530 --> 00:22:55.660
we would love to hear that.

00:22:55.660 --> 00:22:57.470
We really want to make
Kaggle a great tool.

00:22:57.470 --> 00:23:00.280
I do almost all of my data
science work in Kaggle

00:23:00.280 --> 00:23:03.490
and personally would like it to
be better for selfish reasons.

00:23:03.490 --> 00:23:04.900
I think it's already very good.

00:23:04.900 --> 00:23:10.360
But I'd love to hear what else
we can do to help you guys.

00:23:10.360 --> 00:23:11.070
All right.

00:23:11.070 --> 00:23:14.210
And also, there is
feedback in the app.

00:23:14.210 --> 00:23:17.780
So I'm going to wrap it up,
and we can take questions.

00:23:17.780 --> 00:23:21.080
[APPLAUSE]

